theory.intro:
When the region between $y=f(x)$ and the $x$-axis is made of rectangles, triangles, trapezia or parts of a circle, a definite integral can be found from area formulas alone. Area below the $x$-axis counts as negative.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\fill[gray!35] (0.5,0) -- (0.5,2.5) -- (3,0) -- cycle;
\fill[gray!35] (3,0) -- (5.5,-2.5) -- (5.5,0) -- cycle;
\draw[->] (-0.8,0) -- (6.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-3.6) -- (0,4.2) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,<->] (-0.4,3.4) -- (6,-3);
\node at (1.4,3) {$y=f(x)$};
\node at (1.4,0.7) {$+$};
\node at (4.6,-0.9) {$-$};
\node[below] at (0.5,-0.12) {$a$};
\node[below] at (5.5,-0.12) {$b$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

